00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:48,500
Some memories are best forgotten.

2
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Memento is a mystery, thriller film directed by Christopher Nolan.

3
00:14:07,500 --> 00:14:13,500
Leonard Shelby is tracking down the man who raped and murdered his wife.

4
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:56,000
The difficulty of locating his wife's killer, however, is compounded by the fact that
he suffers from a rare, untreatable form of short-term memory loss.

5
00:23:32,500 --> 00:23:38,500
Although he can recall details of life before his accident, Leonard cannot
remember what happened fifteen minutes ago, where he's going, or why.

6
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:21,000
Leonard, played by Guy Pearce.

7
00:32:57,500 --> 00:33:03,500
Natalie, played by Carrie-Anne Moss.

8
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:46,000
Teddy, played by Joe Pantoliano.

9
00:42:22,500 --> 00:42:28,500
Burt, played by Mark Boone Junior.

10
00:47:05,000 --> 00:47:11,000
Waiter, played by Russ Fega.

11
00:51:47,500 --> 00:51:53,500
Leonard's Wife, played by Jorja Fox.

12
00:56:30,000 --> 00:56:36,000
Memento is a 2000 American psychological thriller film written and directed by Christopher Nolan, based on the
short story "Memento Mori" by his brother Jonathan Nolan, which was later published in 2001.

13
01:01:12,500 --> 01:01:18,500
The film stars Guy Pearce, Carrie-Anne Moss, and Joe Pantoliano.

14
01:05:55,000 --> 01:06:01,000
It follows Leonard Shelby (Pearce), a man who suffers from anterograde amnesia—resulting in short-term memory loss and the inability to form new memories—who uses an
elaborate system of photographs, handwritten notes, and tattoos in an attempt to uncover the perpetrator who killed his wife and caused him to sustain the condition.

15
01:10:37,500 --> 01:10:43,500
The film's non-linear narrative is presented as two different sequences of scenes interspersed during the film: a series in black-and-white that is shown
chronologically, and a series of color sequences shown in reverse order (simulating for the audience the mental state of the protagonist).

16
01:15:20,000 --> 01:15:26,000
The two sequences meet at the end of the film,
producing one complete and cohesive narrative.

17
01:20:02,500 --> 01:20:08,500
Memento premiered at the Venice Film Festival on September 5, 2000, and was
theatrically released in the United States on March 16, 2001.

18
01:24:45,000 --> 01:24:51,000
It was acclaimed by critics, who praised its nonlinear structure and
themes of memory, perception, grief, and self-deception.

19
01:29:27,500 --> 01:29:33,500
It was also a commercial success, earning $40 million over its
$9 million budget and gained a cult following.

20
01:34:10,000 --> 01:34:16,000
Memento received many accolades, including the Waldo Salt Screenwriting Award at the Sundance Film
Festival, and Academy Award nominations for Best Original Screenplay and Best Film Editing.

21
01:38:52,500 --> 01:38:58,500
In 2017, the United States Library of Congress deemed the film "culturally, historically, or
aesthetically significant" and selected it for preservation in the National Film Registry.

22
01:43:35,000 --> 01:43:41,000
The film explores themes of drug dealer, amnesia, insulin, tattoo, waitress.

23
01:48:17,500 --> 01:48:23,500
The tone is intense and dark and mind_bending.
